{"tool_name":"ipyphe","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"ipyphe","home":"https://github.com/Bahler-Lab/pyphe","license":"MIT","summary":"Python toolbox for assessing microbial growth and cell viability in high-throughput colony screens.\n\nA python toolbox for phenotype analysis of arrayed microbial colonies written by Stephan Kamrad (stephan.kamrad at crick.ac.uk).\n\nPython toolbox for phenotype analysis of arrayed microbial colonies.","addition_date":"2021-01-18T09:43:03Z","last_update_date":"2021-02-11T09:51:45Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:ipyphe","credit":[{"email":"j.bahler@ucl.ac.uk","name":"J\u00fcrg B\u00e4hler","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4036-1532","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"markus.ralser@crick.ac.uk","name":"Markus Ralser","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9535-7413","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Aggregation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3436"},{"term":"Quantification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3799"},{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"}]}],"name":"ipyphe","owner":"Niclaskn","publication":[{"doi":"10.7554/ELIFE.55160","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 Kamrad et al.Microbial fitness screens are a key technique in functional genomics. We present an all-in-one solution, pyphe, for automating and improving data analysis pipelines associated with large-scale fitness screens, including image acquisition and quantification, data normalisation, and statistical analysis. Pyphe is versatile and processes fitness data from colony sizes, viability scores from phloxine B staining or colony growth curves, all obtained with inexpensive transilluminating flatbed scanners. We apply pyphe to show that the fitness information contained in late endpoint measurements of colony sizes is similar to maximum growth slopes from time series. We phenotype gene-deletion strains of fission yeast in 59,350 individual fitness assays in 70 conditions, revealing that colony size and viability provide complementary, independent information. Viability scores obtained from quantifying the redness of phloxine-stained colonies accurately reflect the fraction of live cells within colonies. Pyphe is user-friendly, open-source and fully documented, illustrated by applications to diverse fitness analysis scenarios.","authors":[{"name":"Bahler J."},{"name":"Correia-Melo C."},{"name":"Cotobal C."},{"name":"Kamrad S."},{"name":"Ralser M."},{"name":"Rodriguez-Lopez M."}],"citationCount":2,"date":"2020-06-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"eLife","title":"Pyphe, a python toolbox for assessing microbial growth and cell viability in high-throughput colony screens"},"pmcid":"PMC7297533","pmid":"32543370"}],"topic":[{"term":"Cytometry","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3934"},{"term":"Imaging","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3382"},{"term":"Infectious disease","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3324"},{"term":"Microbiology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3301"},{"term":"Phenomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3298"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"ipyphe","home":"https://bio.tools/ipyphe","license":"MIT","summary":"Python toolbox for assessing microbial growth and cell viability in high-throughput colony screens.\n\nA python toolbox for phenotype analysis of arrayed microbial colonies written by Stephan Kamrad (stephan.kamrad at crick.ac.uk).\n\nPython toolbox for phenotype analysis of arrayed microbial colonies.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:ipyphe/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,